  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis relies on secondary data and focuses on a single, highly-developed corridor in India's National Capital Region, which may limit its generalizability to less prominent transport axes.",
      "what": "This study models the spatial distribution of industries along a 127-kilometer stretch of India's NH-48 corridor from Gurugram to Behror across four policy phases spanning pre-1991 to 2025. Using exponential growth modeling and distance-decay analysis, it reveals that large-scale industries cluster tightly within 3 km of the highway with a steep decay coefficient of 1.57, while medium-scale industries diffuse further up to 7 km with a lower decay coefficient of 0.68.",
      "why": "It quantifies how industrial scale dictates spatial clustering along major transport corridors, demonstrating that infrastructure proximity exerts a much tighter spatial constraint on large-scale manufacturing than on medium-scale enterprises."
